Finding: {"title":"Constraint-solver occupancy helpers compare only entityId, not entityType","severity":"LOW","area":"Scheduling correctness — solver invariant","files":["src/server/routes/routes-ai-schedule.ts"],"detail":"isEntityFree, entityHoursOnDate, entityEventCount, and studentLessonsOnDate (lines 2185-2250) all filter occupancy slots by `slot.entityId !== entityId` alone; entityType is stored on the slot but never checked. If a student, instructor, and aircraft/equipment row ever shared the same UUID, these helpers would report false conflicts/occupancy across entity types.","impact":"Purely theoretical under normal UUID generation (collision probability negligible), but the invariant is unenforced in code — a future refactor introducing non-UUID or human-chosen IDs would silently break scheduling correctness with no compiler or runtime signal.","recommendation":"Add `slot.entityType === expectedType` to each comparison for defense-in-depth; cheap fix, removes a latent correctness assumption.","confidence":"medium"}
confirmed=true only if the defect genuinely exists. If misread → verdict="false-positive". If real but wrong severity → set correctedSeverity. If real but underspecified → verdict="needs-detail" (kept). Return JSON per the schema.

